  subject_verb_agreement:
    interference_patterns:
    - pattern: Omission of third-person -s
      example: She have three sister.
      frequency: 5
      persistence: 5
      communicative_impact: 4
      example_l1: Dia punya tiga saudara perempuan
      example_gloss: She has three sister
      example_wrong: She have three sister and two brother.
      example_correct: She has three sisters and two brothers.
      explanation: M/I verbs are not marked for person, tense or number. Third-person -s is frequently omitted.
      etiology: interlingual
      flagged: false
      tier: 3
    - pattern: Singular/plural demonstrative confusion
      example: this pictures, these bicycle
      frequency: 3
      persistence: 3
      communicative_impact: 2
      example_l1: gambar ini, sepeda ini
      example_gloss: picture this, bicycle this
      example_wrong: this pictures / these bicycle
      example_correct: these pictures / this bicycle
      explanation: Singular and plural forms of quantifiers and demonstratives are not always correctly used. M/I ini/itu do not change for number.
      etiology: interlingual
      flagged: false
      tier: 3
    - pattern: Quantifier agreement errors
      example: too much corrections, a lots of types
      frequency: 3
      persistence: 3
      communicative_impact: 2
      example_l1: terlalu banyak koreksi, banyak jenis
      example_gloss: too many correction, many type
      example_wrong: The teacher gave too much corrections. / a lots of types of stones
      example_correct: The teacher gave too many corrections. / a lot of types of stones
      explanation: M/I does not distinguish much/many grammatically. Quantifier forms may be confused.
      etiology: interlingual
      flagged: false
      tier: 3
    examples:
    - wrong: She have three sister and two brother.
      correct: She has three sisters and two brothers.
    - wrong: this pictures
      correct: these pictures
    - wrong: these bicycle
      correct: this bicycle
    - wrong: The teacher gave too much corrections.
      correct: The teacher gave too many corrections.
    - wrong: a lots of types of stones
      correct: a lot of types of stones
    why_it_happens: M/I verbs are not inflected for person, tense or number. Third-person -s is the most persistent error. Demonstratives and quantifiers do not change for number in M/I. (Swan & Smith, pp. 282-283)
    teacher_tips:
      how_to_explain: Teach the 'he/she/it + verb-s' rule as non-negotiable. Drill with third-person subjects. Teach this/these and much/many distinctions.
      where_to_start: Start with third-person -s. Then demonstratives. Then quantifiers.
      sequencing: 'Week 1: Third-person -s. Week 2: Demonstratives (this/these). Week 3: Quantifiers (much/many). Week 4: Review.'
      exercises:
      - name: subject_verb_agreement Audit
        type: error-correction
        description: 20 sentences with SVA errors from M/I learner writing.
        duration: 15
      - name: subject_verb_agreement Translation
        type: translation
        description: Translate 15 sentences focusing on SVA.
        duration: 15
      - name: subject_verb_agreement Practice
        type: fill-in-the-blank
        description: 20 sentences practicing SVA.
        duration: 10
      - name: subject_verb_agreement Error Hunt
        type: error-correction
        description: A paragraph with 10 SVA errors.
        duration: 15
    sources:
    - Swan & Smith Learner English (Indonesian)
    - Parrott Grammar for English Language Teachers
    source_count: 2
    source_type: textbook
    frequency: 5
    persistence: 5
    communicative_impact: 4
    flagged: false
    tier: 1
    source_rank: 1
    citations:
    - claim: M/I verbs are not marked for person, tense or number; third-person -s omitted
      source: Swan & Smith (2001) Learner English, 2nd ed., Cambridge University Press
      location: Malay/Indonesian speakers, p. 282
      tier: 1
    - claim: Singular and plural forms of quantifiers and demonstratives not always correctly used
      source: Swan & Smith (2001) Learner English, 2nd ed., Cambridge University Press
      location: Malay/Indonesian speakers, p. 283
      tier: 1
  prepositions:
    interference_patterns:
    - pattern: Preposition transfer from M/I (at Monday)
      example: He don't work at Monday.
      frequency: 4
      persistence: 4
      communicative_impact: 3
      example_l1: Dia tidak bekerja pada hari Senin
      example_gloss: He not work on day Monday
      example_wrong: He don't work at Monday.
      example_correct: He doesn't work on Monday.
      explanation: M/I uses pada for time expressions. English uses different prepositions for days (on), months (in), and times (at).
      etiology: interlingual
      flagged: false
      tier: 3
    - pattern: Preposition omission or wrong choice after verbs/adjectives
      example: I have to attend for my garden.
      frequency: 4
      persistence: 4
      communicative_impact: 3
      example_l1: Saya harus mengurus taman saya
      example_gloss: I must tend garden my
      example_wrong: I have to attend for my garden. / He shouted in angry his mother.
      example_correct: I have to attend to my garden. / He shouted angrily at his mother.
      explanation: Errors occur in the use of the correct preposition after a verb, adjective or noun. Many English prepositions have no direct M/I equivalents.
      etiology: interlingual
      flagged: false
      tier: 3
    - pattern: Adverbial particle errors (on/off confusion)
      example: Please don't on and off the light.
      frequency: 3
      persistence: 3
      communicative_impact: 2
      example_l1: Tolong jangan matikan dan nyalakan lampu terus-menerus
      example_gloss: Please not turn-off and turn-on light continuously
      example_wrong: Please don't on and off the light.
      example_correct: Please don't turn the light on and off.
      explanation: Similar problems arise with adverbial particles. M/I expresses these concepts differently.
      etiology: interlingual
      flagged: false
      tier: 3
    - pattern: Preposition in fixed expressions
      example: He roundabout his house with bicycle.
      frequency: 3
      persistence: 3
      communicative_impact: 2
      example_l1: Dia mengelilingi rumahnya dengan sepeda
      example_gloss: He go-around his house with bicycle
      example_wrong: He roundabout his house with bicycle.
      example_correct: He went around his house by bicycle.
      explanation: Learners have difficulty choosing the right preposition in fixed expressions like by bicycle, on the radio, by the back door.
      etiology: interlingual
      flagged: false
      tier: 3
    examples:
    - wrong: He don't work at Monday.
      correct: He doesn't work on Monday.
    - wrong: I have to attend for my garden.
      correct: I have to attend to my garden.
    - wrong: He shouted in angry his mother.
      correct: He shouted angrily at his mother.
    - wrong: Please don't on and off the light.
      correct: Please don't turn the light on and off.
    - wrong: He roundabout his house with bicycle.
      correct: He went around his house by bicycle.
    why_it_happens: Many English prepositions do not have equivalents in M/I and their functions are performed by other types of word. Errors occur in the use of the correct preposition after a verb, adjective or noun. (Swan & Smith, p. 290)
    teacher_tips:
      how_to_explain: Teach common M/I-English preposition differences. Drill fixed expressions. Teach prepositions as vocabulary items that must be memorised.
      where_to_start: Start with time prepositions (at/on/in). Then place prepositions. Then verb+preposition combinations.
      sequencing: 'Week 1: Time prepositions. Week 2: Place prepositions. Week 3: Verb+preposition. Week 4: Fixed expressions.'
      exercises:
      - name: prepositions Audit
        type: error-correction
        description: 20 sentences with prepositions errors from M/I learner writing.
        duration: 15
      - name: prepositions Translation
        type: translation
        description: Translate 15 sentences focusing on prepositions.
        duration: 15
      - name: prepositions Practice
        type: fill-in-the-blank
        description: 20 sentences practicing prepositions.
        duration: 10
      - name: prepositions Error Hunt
        type: error-correction
        description: A paragraph with 10 prepositions errors.
        duration: 15
    sources:
    - Swan & Smith Learner English (Indonesian)
    - Parrott Grammar for English Language Teachers
