  passive_voice:
    interference_patterns:
    - pattern: Avoidance of passive voice (M/I prefers active)
      example: That car drives by Amin.
      frequency: 4
      persistence: 4
      communicative_impact: 3
      example_l1: Mobil itu dikendarai oleh Amin
      example_gloss: Car that driven by Amin
      example_wrong: That car drives by Amin.
      example_correct: That car is driven by Amin.
      explanation: M/I has a strong preference for active forms. In spoken M/I the passive is generally avoided. Learners rephrase or avoid English passives.
      etiology: interlingual
      flagged: false
      tier: 3
    - pattern: Incorrect passive formation
      example: The door is open by me.
      frequency: 3
      persistence: 3
      communicative_impact: 3
      example_l1: Pintu itu dibuka oleh saya
      example_gloss: Door that opened by me
      example_wrong: The door is open by me. / For Sarimah the coffee make by me.
      example_correct: The door was opened by me. / For Sarimah the coffee was made by me.
      explanation: Passives are formed very differently in M/I (using di- prefix). Learners produce incorrect English passive forms.
      etiology: interlingual
      flagged: false
      tier: 3
    examples:
    - wrong: That car drives by Amin.
      correct: That car is driven by Amin.
    - wrong: The door is open by me.
      correct: The door was opened by me.
    - wrong: For Sarimah the coffee make by me.
      correct: For Sarimah the coffee was made by me.
    why_it_happens: M/I has a strong preference for active forms. In spoken M/I the passive is generally avoided. Passives are formed very differently in the two languages (M/I uses di- prefix). (Swan & Smith, p. 288)
    teacher_tips:
      how_to_explain: 'Teach the passive formula: be + past participle. M/I uses di- prefix; English uses a different structure.'
      where_to_start: Start with the passive formula. Then teach when to use passive (focus on the action, not the agent).
      sequencing: 'Week 1: Passive formula (be + p.p.). Week 2: Active vs passive choice. Week 3: Passive in academic writing. Week 4: Review.'
      exercises:
      - name: passive_voice Audit
        type: error-correction
        description: 20 sentences with passive voice errors from M/I learner writing.
        duration: 15
      - name: passive_voice Translation
        type: translation
        description: Translate 15 sentences focusing on passive voice.
        duration: 15
      - name: passive_voice Practice
        type: fill-in-the-blank
        description: 20 sentences practicing passive voice.
        duration: 10
      - name: passive_voice Error Hunt
        type: error-correction
        description: A paragraph with 10 passive voice errors.
        duration: 15
    sources:
    - Swan & Smith Learner English (Indonesian)
    - Parrott Grammar for English Language Teachers
    source_count: 2
    source_type: textbook
    frequency: 4
    persistence: 4
    communicative_impact: 3
    flagged: false
    tier: 1
    source_rank: 1
    citations:
    - claim: M/I has a strong preference for active forms; passive generally avoided in speech
      source: Swan & Smith (2001) Learner English, 2nd ed., Cambridge University Press
      location: Malay/Indonesian speakers, p. 288
      tier: 1
    - claim: Passives formed very differently in M/I (di- prefix); learners produce incorrect forms
      source: Swan & Smith (2001) Learner English, 2nd ed., Cambridge University Press
      location: Malay/Indonesian speakers, p. 288
      tier: 1
  tense_aspect:
    interference_patterns:
    - pattern: Simple past for present perfect (sudah/telah/pernah)
      example: I write the letter yesterday.
      frequency: 5
      persistence: 5
      communicative_impact: 4
      example_l1: Saya menulis surat kemarin
      example_gloss: I write letter yesterday
      example_wrong: I write the letter yesterday. / I was write yesterday the letter. / I finished written the letter.
      example_correct: I wrote the letter yesterday. / I wrote the letter yesterday. / I finished writing the letter.
      explanation: M/I has nothing corresponding to the English distinction between simple past and present perfect. Sudah/telah/pernah indicates completion but maps to both.
      etiology: interlingual
      flagged: false
      tier: 3
    - pattern: Present tense with time adverb for progressive
      example: Now he swim.
      frequency: 4
      persistence: 4
      communicative_impact: 3
      example_l1: Sekarang dia berenang
      example_gloss: Now he swim
      example_wrong: Now he swim. / He is swim at this time. / He swim right now.
      example_correct: He is swimming now. / He is swimming at this time. / He is swimming right now.
      explanation: M/I uses the temporal adverb sekarang (now) before or after a verb to signify an ongoing action. Learners transfer this to English without the -ing form.
      etiology: interlingual
      flagged: false
      tier: 3
    - pattern: Future time expressed without will/going to
      example: Tomorrow you come.
      frequency: 4
      persistence: 4
      communicative_impact: 3
      example_l1: Besok kamu datang
      example_gloss: Tomorrow you come
      example_wrong: Tomorrow you come. / This book will we read. / They will to market on Sunday.
      example_correct: Tomorrow you will come. / We will read this book. / They will go to the market on Sunday.
      explanation: The aspectual particle akan in M/I indicates future time. Students have problems using and distinguishing the various English future forms.
      etiology: interlingual
      flagged: false
      tier: 3
    - pattern: Progressive/continuous without auxiliary
      example: They eating their dinner tonight.
      frequency: 5
      persistence: 5
      communicative_impact: 4
      example_l1: Mereka makan malam ini
      example_gloss: They eat dinner this-night
      example_wrong: They eating their dinner tonight. / They coming back late tomorrow. / They waiting here since 2 o'clock past.
      example_correct: They are eating their dinner tonight. / They are coming back late tomorrow. / They have been waiting here since 2 o'clock.
      explanation: M/I expresses durative aspect using sedang/semasa/sewaktu/sementara with a verb. Learners use the English -ing form but drop the auxiliary.
      etiology: interlingual
      flagged: false
      tier: 3
    examples:
    - wrong: I write the letter yesterday.
      correct: I wrote the letter yesterday.
    - wrong: Last week I have wrote to her.
      correct: Last week I wrote to her.
    - wrong: I already finish write the notes.
      correct: I have already finished writing the notes.
    - wrong: Now he swim.
      correct: He is swimming now.
    - wrong: Tomorrow you come.
      correct: Tomorrow you will come.
    - wrong: They eating their dinner tonight.
      correct: They are eating their dinner tonight.
    - wrong: They playing chess yesterday when the phone was ringing.
      correct: They were playing chess yesterday when the phone rang.
    why_it_happens: M/I verbs are not marked for person, tense or number. Auxiliary elements indicate tense and aspect. M/I has nothing corresponding to the English distinction between simple past and present perfect. The progressive is expressed with sedang + verb without auxiliary. (Swan & Smith, pp. 286-287)
    teacher_tips:
      how_to_explain: 'Teach: English verbs change for tense. Past = -ed (usually). Progressive = be + -ing. Future = will + base verb. M/I uses particles; English changes the verb.'
      where_to_start: Start with past tense. Then present perfect vs simple past. Then progressive. Then future.
      sequencing: 'Week 1: Past tense. Week 2: Present perfect vs past. Week 3: Progressive (be + -ing). Week 4: Future forms.'
      exercises:
      - name: tense_aspect Audit
        type: error-correction
        description: 20 sentences with tense/aspect errors from M/I learner writing.
        duration: 15
      - name: tense_aspect Translation
        type: translation
        description: Translate 15 sentences focusing on tense/aspect.
        duration: 15
      - name: tense_aspect Practice
        type: fill-in-the-blank
        description: 20 sentences practicing tense/aspect.
        duration: 10
      - name: tense_aspect Error Hunt
        type: error-correction
        description: A paragraph with 10 tense/aspect errors.
        duration: 15
    sources:
    - Swan & Smith Learner English (Indonesian)
    - Parrott Grammar for English Language Teachers
