  copula_be:
    interference_patterns:
    - pattern: Omission of copula (to be)
      example: Yusoff very happy.
      frequency: 5
      persistence: 5
      communicative_impact: 4
      example_l1: Yusoff sangat gembira
      example_gloss: Yusoff very happy
      example_wrong: Yusoff very happy. / His sisters very clever too. / They hardworking farmers.
      example_correct: Yusoff is/was very happy. / His sisters are very clever too. / They are hardworking farmers.
      explanation: Berada, adalah and ialah correspond to the English copula to be, but they are frequently optional. The copula is dropped in sentences expressing a condition or state.
      etiology: interlingual
      flagged: false
      tier: 3
    - pattern: Incorrect copula use (number/person errors)
      example: Their parents not happy.
      frequency: 4
      persistence: 4
      communicative_impact: 3
      example_l1: Orang tua mereka tidak senang
      example_gloss: Parents their not happy
      example_wrong: Their parents not happy. / Their parent is not happy. / They was hardworking farmers.
      example_correct: Their parents are not happy. / Their parents are not happy. / They were hardworking farmers.
      explanation: Mother-tongue interference and confusion over which form is correct results in omission of forms of to be, or errors in their use.
      etiology: interlingual
      flagged: false
      tier: 3
    examples:
    - wrong: Yusoff very happy.
      correct: Yusoff is very happy.
    - wrong: His sisters very clever too.
      correct: His sisters are very clever too.
    - wrong: They hardworking farmers.
      correct: They are hardworking farmers.
    - wrong: Their parents not happy.
      correct: Their parents are not happy.
    why_it_happens: Berada, adalah and ialah correspond to the English copula to be, but they are frequently optional. The copula is dropped in sentences expressing a condition or state of existence. (Swan & Smith, pp. 287-288)
    teacher_tips:
      how_to_explain: 'Teach: English ALWAYS needs a verb. After a subject + adjective, you need ''be'' (am/is/are/was/were). M/I can drop this; English cannot.'
      where_to_start: Start with subject + be + adjective. Then subject + be + noun. Then subject + be + location.
      sequencing: 'Week 1: am/is/are with adjectives. Week 2: was/were (past). Week 3: be + noun/location. Week 4: Review.'
      exercises:
      - name: copula_be Audit
        type: error-correction
        description: 20 sentences with copula be errors from M/I learner writing.
        duration: 15
      - name: copula_be Translation
        type: translation
        description: Translate 15 sentences focusing on copula be.
        duration: 15
      - name: copula_be Practice
        type: fill-in-the-blank
        description: 20 sentences practicing copula be.
        duration: 10
      - name: copula_be Error Hunt
        type: error-correction
        description: A paragraph with 10 copula be errors.
        duration: 15
    sources:
    - Swan & Smith Learner English (Indonesian)
    - Parrott Grammar for English Language Teachers
    source_count: 2
    source_type: textbook
    frequency: 5
    persistence: 5
    communicative_impact: 4
    flagged: false
    tier: 1
    source_rank: 1
    citations:
    - claim: Berada/adalah/ialah correspond to English copula but are frequently optional
      source: Swan & Smith (2001) Learner English, 2nd ed., Cambridge University Press
      location: Malay/Indonesian speakers, pp. 287-288
      tier: 1
    - claim: Copula dropped in sentences expressing condition or state of existence
      source: Swan & Smith (2001) Learner English, 2nd ed., Cambridge University Press
      location: Malay/Indonesian speakers, p. 287
      tier: 1
  relative_clauses:
    interference_patterns:
    - pattern: Use of 'which' for people (yang = who/which/that)
      example: People which live in town are rich.
      frequency: 4
      persistence: 4
      communicative_impact: 3
      example_l1: Orang yang tinggal di kota itu kaya
      example_gloss: Person who/which live in town that rich
      example_wrong: People which live in town are rich.
      example_correct: People who live in town are rich.
      explanation: M/I has only one relative word yang, which corresponds to English who, which, that and zero. Learners default to which for all antecedents.
      etiology: interlingual
      flagged: false
      tier: 3
    - pattern: Relative pronoun duplicated by personal pronoun
      example: The letter that I haven't received it yet.
      frequency: 4
      persistence: 4
      communicative_impact: 3
      example_l1: Surat yang saya belum terima itu
      example_gloss: Letter that I not-yet receive that
      example_wrong: The letter that I haven't received it yet.
      example_correct: The letter that I haven't received yet.
      explanation: Relative pronouns may be duplicated by personal pronouns, a common M/I discourse pattern.
      etiology: interlingual
      flagged: false
      tier: 3
    - pattern: Adjective used after relative pronoun (no copula)
      example: The flower that red is hibiscus.
      frequency: 3
      persistence: 3
      communicative_impact: 2
      example_l1: Bunga yang merah itu bunga raya
      example_gloss: Flower that red that hibiscus
      example_wrong: The flower that red is hibiscus.
      example_correct: The flower that is red is hibiscus.
      explanation: M/I yang can be followed directly by an adjective without a copula. English requires the copula after the relative pronoun.
      etiology: interlingual
      flagged: false
      tier: 3
    examples:
    - wrong: People which live in town are rich.
      correct: People who live in town are rich.
    - wrong: The letter that I haven't received it yet.
      correct: The letter that I haven't received yet.
    - wrong: The flower that red is hibiscus.
      correct: The flower that is red is hibiscus.
    why_it_happens: M/I has only one relative word yang, which corresponds to English who, which, that and zero. Relative pronouns may be duplicated by personal pronouns. (Swan & Smith, pp. 283-284)
    teacher_tips:
      how_to_explain: 'Teach: For people -> who/that. For things -> that/which. Do NOT repeat the pronoun after the relative clause. Include the copula if needed.'
      where_to_start: Start with who vs which/that distinction. Then teach pronoun duplication error. Then copula in relative clauses.
      sequencing: 'Week 1: who/which/that. Week 2: Pronoun duplication error. Week 3: Copula in relative clauses. Week 4: Review.'
      exercises:
      - name: relative_clauses Audit
        type: error-correction
        description: 20 sentences with relative clause errors from M/I learner writing.
        duration: 15
      - name: relative_clauses Translation
        type: translation
        description: Translate 15 sentences focusing on relative clauses.
        duration: 15
      - name: relative_clauses Practice
        type: fill-in-the-blank
        description: 20 sentences practicing relative clauses.
        duration: 10
      - name: relative_clauses Error Hunt
        type: error-correction
        description: A paragraph with 10 relative clause errors.
        duration: 15
    sources:
    - Swan & Smith Learner English (Indonesian)
    - Parrott Grammar for English Language Teachers
