      source: Swan & Smith (2001) Learner English, 2nd ed., Cambridge University Press
      location: Malay/Indonesian speakers, p. 289
      tier: 1
  comparatives:
    interference_patterns:
    - pattern: Use of 'as' instead of 'than' after comparatives
      example: My sister as clever your sister.
      frequency: 4
      persistence: 4
      communicative_impact: 3
      example_l1: Saya kakak sepintar kakak kamu
      example_gloss: My sister as-clever your sister
      example_wrong: My sister as clever your sister.
      example_correct: My sister is as clever as your sister.
      explanation: Common errors with comparatives point to direct translation from M/I. M/I uses 'se-' prefix or 'sama...dengan' for equality comparisons.
      etiology: interlingual
      flagged: false
      tier: 3
    - pattern: Double comparative/superlative
      example: His cousin is very bravest soldier.
      frequency: 3
      persistence: 3
      communicative_impact: 2
      example_l1: Sepupunya adalah pemberani yang paling berani
      example_gloss: His-counin is bravest
      example_wrong: His cousin is very bravest soldier. / His house less smaller than your house.
      example_correct: His cousin is the bravest soldier. / His house is smaller than your house.
      explanation: M/I forms comparatives differently (using 'lebih' for more and '-est' equivalents). Learners produce double comparatives/superlatives.
      etiology: interlingual
      flagged: false
      tier: 3
    - pattern: Superlative with 'most' + '-est' form
      example: Pak Toha most richest than all people.
      frequency: 3
      persistence: 3
      communicative_impact: 2
      example_l1: Di desa saya, Pak Toha paling kaya dari semua orang
      example_gloss: In village my, Pak Toha most rich from all people
      example_wrong: In my village, Pak Toha most richest than all people.
      example_correct: In my village, Pak Toha is the richest of all people.
      explanation: M/I uses 'paling' for superlative. Learners combine 'most' with '-est' form, producing double superlatives.
      etiology: interlingual
      flagged: false
      tier: 3
    examples:
    - wrong: My sister as clever your sister.
      correct: My sister is as clever as your sister.
    - wrong: His cousin is very bravest soldier.
      correct: His cousin is the bravest soldier.
    - wrong: His house less smaller than your house.
      correct: His house is smaller than your house.
    - wrong: In my village, Pak Toha most richest than all people.
      correct: In my village, Pak Toha is the richest of all people.
    why_it_happens: Common errors with adjectives describing degrees of comparison point to direct translation from M/I. M/I forms comparatives using 'lebih' (more) and 'paling' (most) rather than -er/-est suffixes. (Swan & Smith, p. 290)
    teacher_tips:
      how_to_explain: 'Teach: 1-2 syllable adjectives -> -er/-est. 3+ syllable adjectives -> more/most. Do NOT use both (no double comparatives). As...as for equality comparisons.'
      where_to_start: Start with -er/-est rule. Then teach more/most. Then teach as...as. Then address double comparative errors.
      sequencing: 'Week 1: -er/-est. Week 2: more/most. Week 3: as...as. Week 4: Review.'
      exercises:
      - name: comparatives Audit
        type: error-correction
        description: 20 sentences with comparatives errors from M/I learner writing.
        duration: 15
      - name: comparatives Translation
        type: translation
        description: Translate 15 sentences focusing on comparatives.
        duration: 15
      - name: comparatives Practice
        type: fill-in-the-blank
        description: 20 sentences practicing comparatives.
        duration: 10
      - name: comparatives Error Hunt
        type: error-correction
        description: A paragraph with 10 comparatives errors.
        duration: 15
    sources:
    - Swan & Smith Learner English (Indonesian)
    - Parrott Grammar for English Language Teachers
