  modal_verbs:
    interference_patterns:
    - pattern: '''Can'' overused for multiple modal meanings'
      example: You can drive, isn't it?
      frequency: 5
      persistence: 5
      communicative_impact: 4
      example_l1: Kamu bisa mengemudi, bukan?
      example_gloss: You can drive, not
      example_wrong: You can drive, isn't it? / Can you lend me your car or not? / She can lend you her car.
      example_correct: You can drive, can't you? / Can you lend me your car? / She will lend you her car.
      explanation: The complex English modal system is drastically reduced in M/I to two main modals, can and must. Can takes on a wide range of uses (ability, permission, willingness, possibility).
      etiology: interlingual
      flagged: false
      tier: 3
    - pattern: '''Must be'' for certainty without inversion'
      example: Must be he is sick.
      frequency: 3
      persistence: 3
      communicative_impact: 2
      example_l1: Pasti dia sakit
      example_gloss: Must he sick
      example_wrong: Must be he is sick. / Cannot be he so rich as this.
      example_correct: He must be sick. / He cannot be as rich as this.
      explanation: M/I word order for modal expressions differs. Learners place 'must be' at the beginning without inversion.
      etiology: interlingual
      flagged: false
      tier: 3
    - pattern: Modal avoidance; adverbs used instead
      example: Maybe Ahmad calling on the phone.
      frequency: 3
      persistence: 3
      communicative_impact: 2
      example_l1: Mungkin Ahmad menelepon
      example_gloss: Maybe Ahmad calling-phone
      example_wrong: Maybe Ahmad calling on the phone. / I think Samy joking. / Surely that Fatimah.
      example_correct: That will be Ahmad calling on the phone. / Samy could be joking. / That has to be Fatimah.
      explanation: Learners may avoid modals altogether, using adverbs or ordinary verbs to express modal meanings.
      etiology: interlingual
      flagged: false
      tier: 3
    examples:
    - wrong: You can drive, isn't it?
      correct: You can drive, can't you?
    - wrong: Can you lend me your car or not?
      correct: Can you lend me your car?
    - wrong: He can't be sleeping all this time.
      correct: He can't have been sleeping all this time.
    - wrong: Must be he is sick.
      correct: He must be sick.
    - wrong: Cannot be he so rich as this.
      correct: He cannot be as rich as this.
    - wrong: Maybe Ahmad calling on the phone.
      correct: That will be Ahmad calling on the phone.
    - wrong: You must go see your professor.
      correct: You must go and see your professor.
    why_it_happens: The complex modal auxiliary system of standard English is drastically reduced in M/I to two main modals, can and must. Can in particular takes on a wide range of uses. Learners may also avoid modals altogether. (Swan & Smith, pp. 288-289)
    teacher_tips:
      how_to_explain: 'Teach modal hierarchy: can (ability/permission), may (permission/possibility), will (future/willingness), must (obligation/certainty), should (advice). M/I mainly uses ''bisa'' and ''harus''.'
      where_to_start: Start with can vs may vs will. Then must for certainty. Then modal avoidance patterns.
      sequencing: 'Week 1: can/may. Week 2: will/would. Week 3: must/should. Week 4: Modal avoidance and adverbs.'
      exercises:
      - name: modal_verbs Audit
        type: error-correction
        description: 20 sentences with modal verbs errors from M/I learner writing.
        duration: 15
      - name: modal_verbs Translation
        type: translation
        description: Translate 15 sentences focusing on modal verbs.
        duration: 15
      - name: modal_verbs Practice
        type: fill-in-the-blank
        description: 20 sentences practicing modal verbs.
        duration: 10
      - name: modal_verbs Error Hunt
        type: error-correction
        description: A paragraph with 10 modal verbs errors.
        duration: 15
    sources:
    - Swan & Smith Learner English (Indonesian)
    - Parrott Grammar for English Language Teachers
    source_count: 2
    source_type: textbook
    frequency: 5
    persistence: 5
    communicative_impact: 4
    flagged: false
    tier: 1
    source_rank: 1
    citations:
    - claim: English modal system drastically reduced in M/I to can and must
      source: Swan & Smith (2001) Learner English, 2nd ed., Cambridge University Press
      location: Malay/Indonesian speakers, pp. 288-289
      tier: 1
    - claim: Can takes on wide range of uses in M/I English (ability, permission, willingness, etc.)
      source: Swan & Smith (2001) Learner English, 2nd ed., Cambridge University Press
      location: Malay/Indonesian speakers, p. 288
      tier: 1
    - claim: Learners may avoid modals altogether, using adverbs instead
      source: Swan & Smith (2001) Learner English, 2nd ed., Cambridge University Press
      location: Malay/Indonesian speakers, pp. 288-289
      tier: 1
  gerund_infinitive:
    interference_patterns:
    - pattern: Gerund/infinitive confusion (to + -ing)
      example: To running very good for your health.
      frequency: 4
      persistence: 4
      communicative_impact: 3
      example_l1: Berlari sangat baik untuk kesehatan
      example_gloss: Running very good for health
      example_wrong: To running very good for your health. / My mother like to cooking not to sewing.
      example_correct: Running is very good for your health. / My mother likes cooking, not sewing.
      explanation: Gerunds and infinitives do not exist in M/I. The various English forms are often confused by learners.
      etiology: interlingual
      flagged: false
      tier: 3
    - pattern: Bare infinitive after modals (to continuing)
      example: Without money, I can't to continuing my studies.
      frequency: 4
      persistence: 4
      communicative_impact: 3
      example_l1: Tanpa uang, saya tidak bisa melanjutkan studi
      example_gloss: Without money, I not can continue study
      example_wrong: Without money, I can't to continuing my studies. / Mother is going clean the room. / I shall to go to market early.
      example_correct: Without money, I can't continue my studies. / Mother is going to clean the room. / I shall go to the market early.
      explanation: M/I does not use infinitives. Learners add 'to' after modals or use -ing form incorrectly.
      etiology: interlingual
      flagged: false
      tier: 3
    examples:
    - wrong: To running very good for your health.
      correct: Running is very good for your health.
    - wrong: My mother like to cooking not to sewing.
      correct: My mother likes cooking, not sewing.
    - wrong: Without money, I can't to continuing my studies.
      correct: Without money, I can't continue my studies.
    - wrong: Mother is going clean the room.
      correct: Mother is going to clean the room.
    - wrong: I shall to go to market early.
      correct: I shall go to the market early.
    why_it_happens: Gerunds and infinitives do not exist in M/I. The various English forms are often confused by learners. (Swan & Smith, p. 287)
    teacher_tips:
      how_to_explain: 'Teach: After prepositions -> always gerund (-ing). After modals -> bare infinitive (no ''to''). Some verbs take gerund, some take infinitive. M/I has neither; learners must learn the English system from scratch.'
      where_to_start: Start with gerund after prepositions. Then modal + bare infinitive. Then verb patterns (verb + gerund vs verb + infinitive).
      sequencing: 'Week 1: Gerund after prepositions. Week 2: Modal + bare infinitive. Week 3: Verb + gerund/infinitive patterns. Week 4: Review.'
      exercises:
      - name: gerund_infinitive Audit
        type: error-correction
        description: 20 sentences with gerund/infinitive errors from M/I learner writing.
        duration: 15
      - name: gerund_infinitive Translation
        type: translation
        description: Translate 15 sentences focusing on gerund/infinitive.
        duration: 15
      - name: gerund_infinitive Practice
        type: fill-in-the-blank
        description: 20 sentences practicing gerund/infinitive.
        duration: 10
      - name: gerund_infinitive Error Hunt
        type: error-correction
        description: A paragraph with 10 gerund/infinitive errors.
        duration: 15
    sources:
    - Swan & Smith Learner English (Indonesian)
    - Parrott Grammar for English Language Teachers
    source_count: 2
    source_type: textbook
    frequency: 4
    persistence: 4
    communicative_impact: 3
    flagged: false
    tier: 1
    source_rank: 1
    citations:
    - claim: Gerunds and infinitives do not exist in M/I; forms often confused
      source: Swan & Smith (2001) Learner English, 2nd ed., Cambridge University Press
      location: Malay/Indonesian speakers, p. 287
