  adverbs:
    interference_patterns:
    - pattern: Adjective used as adverb (no -ly)
      example: You sit quiet there.
      frequency: 4
      persistence: 3
      communicative_impact: 2
      example_l1: Kamu duduk diam di sana
      example_gloss: You sit quiet there
      example_wrong: You sit quiet there. / Ali dress smart every day.
      example_correct: You sit quietly there. / Ali dresses smartly every day.
      explanation: Learners may substitute adverbs for adjectives and vice versa, often under the influence of the mother tongue.
      etiology: interlingual
      flagged: false
      tier: 3
    - pattern: Adverb form confusion (adjective vs adverb vs noun)
      example: He write with well.
      frequency: 3
      persistence: 3
      communicative_impact: 2
      example_l1: Dia menulis dengan baik
      example_gloss: He writes with good
      example_wrong: He write with well. / a luxury life / consist of agriculture land
      example_correct: He writes well. / a luxurious life / consisting of agricultural land
      explanation: Confusion with other parts of speech also occurs. M/I does not always distinguish adjectives from adverbs morphologically.
      etiology: interlingual
      flagged: false
      tier: 3
    - pattern: Adverb placement errors
      example: In recent we have the steadily increase of tourists.
      frequency: 3
      persistence: 3
      communicative_impact: 2
      example_l1: Akhir-akhir ini kita punya peningkatan stabil turis
      example_gloss: Recently we have increase steady of tourists
      example_wrong: In recent we have the steadily increase of tourists.
      example_correct: Recently we have seen a steady increase in tourists.
      explanation: M/I adverb placement rules differ from English.
      etiology: interlingual
      flagged: false
      tier: 3
    examples:
    - wrong: You sit quiet there.
      correct: You sit quietly there.
    - wrong: Ali dress smart every day.
      correct: Ali dresses smartly every day.
    - wrong: In recent we have the steadily increase of tourists.
      correct: Recently we have seen a steady increase in tourists.
    - wrong: The students study hardly.
      correct: The students study hard.
    - wrong: He write with well.
      correct: He writes well.
    why_it_happens: Learners may substitute adverbs for adjectives and vice versa, often under the influence of the mother tongue. Confusion with other parts of speech also occurs. (Swan & Smith, pp. 289-290)
    teacher_tips:
      how_to_explain: 'Teach: Adjective describes a noun. Adverb describes a verb. Most adverbs add -ly. Good -> well (irregular). Hard (not hardly).'
      where_to_start: Start with the -ly rule. Then teach exceptions (fast, hard, late). Then teach good vs well.
      sequencing: 'Week 1: -ly adverbs. Week 2: Exceptions (fast, hard, late). Week 3: Good vs well. Week 4: Review.'
      exercises:
      - name: adverbs Audit
        type: error-correction
        description: 20 sentences with adverbs errors from M/I learner writing.
        duration: 15
      - name: adverbs Translation
        type: translation
        description: Translate 15 sentences focusing on adverbs.
        duration: 15
      - name: adverbs Practice
        type: fill-in-the-blank
        description: 20 sentences practicing adverbs.
        duration: 10
      - name: adverbs Error Hunt
        type: error-correction
        description: A paragraph with 10 adverbs errors.
        duration: 15
    sources:
    - Swan & Smith Learner English (Indonesian)
    - Parrott Grammar for English Language Teachers
    source_count: 2
    source_type: textbook
    frequency: 4
    persistence: 3
    communicative_impact: 2
