      tier: 1
    - claim: Confusion with other parts of speech also occurs (adjective vs adverb vs noun)
      source: Swan & Smith (2001) Learner English, 2nd ed., Cambridge University Press
      location: Malay/Indonesian speakers, p. 290
      tier: 1
  possessives:
    interference_patterns:
    - pattern: Possessive pronoun omission or wrong form
      example: my wife friend
      frequency: 4
      persistence: 4
      communicative_impact: 3
      example_l1: istri saya teman
      example_gloss: my wife friend / wife-my friend
      example_wrong: my wife friend / my father name Jaid bin Omar / yours school bag
      explanation: Possessive relations in M/I can be shown by a bound pronoun -nya or by the free personal pronouns. Students find the English system very different.
      etiology: interlingual
      flagged: false
      tier: 3
    - pattern: Of-construction used instead of possessive 's
      example: the book of them
      frequency: 3
      persistence: 3
      communicative_impact: 2
      example_l1: buku mereka
      example_gloss: book them/their
      example_wrong: the book of them / their houses / the cat's tails
      explanation: M/I uses noun + possessor word order. Learners use 'of' constructions or omit the possessive marker.
      etiology: interlingual
      flagged: false
      tier: 3
    examples:
    - wrong: my wife friend
      correct: my wife's friend
    - wrong: my father name Jaid bin Omar.
      correct: my father's name is Jaid bin Omar.
    - wrong: yours school bag
      correct: your school bag
    - wrong: the book of them
      correct: their book
    - wrong: the cat's tails
      correct: the cat's tail
    why_it_happens: Possessive relations in M/I can be shown by a bound pronoun -nya or by the free personal pronouns dia (he/she/it) and mereka (they). Students have difficulty with the very different English system. (Swan & Smith, pp. 283-284)
    teacher_tips:
      how_to_explain: 'Teach: For people/animals, use ''s (the cat''s tail). For things, use of (the leg of the table). Possessive pronouns: my, your, his, her, its, our, their.'
      where_to_start: Start with possessive 's. Then possessive pronouns. Then of-constructions for things.
      sequencing: 'Week 1: Possessive ''s. Week 2: Possessive pronouns. Week 3: Of-constructions. Week 4: Review.'
      exercises:
      - name: possessives Audit
        type: error-correction
        description: 20 sentences with possessives errors from M/I learner writing.
        duration: 15
      - name: possessives Translation
        type: translation
        description: Translate 15 sentences focusing on possessives.
        duration: 15
      - name: possessives Practice
        type: fill-in-the-blank
        description: 20 sentences practicing possessives.
        duration: 10
      - name: possessives Error Hunt
        type: error-correction
        description: A paragraph with 10 possessives errors.
        duration: 15
    sources:
    - Swan & Smith Learner English (Indonesian)
    - Parrott Grammar for English Language Teachers
    source_count: 2
    source_type: textbook
    frequency: 4
    persistence: 4
    communicative_impact: 3
    flagged: false
    tier: 1
    source_rank: 1
    citations:
    - claim: Possessive relations shown by bound pronoun -nya or free pronouns; English system very different
      source: Swan & Smith (2001) Learner English, 2nd ed., Cambridge University Press
      location: Malay/Indonesian speakers, pp. 283-284
      tier: 1
    - claim: Errors include *my wife friend, *the book of them, *your school bag
      source: Swan & Smith (2001) Learner English, 2nd ed., Cambridge University Press
      location: Malay/Indonesian speakers, p. 284
      tier: 1
  existential_there:
    interference_patterns:
    - pattern: Use of 'ada' (got/has/is) instead of 'there is/are'
      example: A few people in the room.
      frequency: 4
      persistence: 4
      communicative_impact: 3
      example_l1: Ada beberapa orang di ruangan
      example_gloss: Got/Has several people in room
      example_wrong: A few people in the room. / Was an examination last week. / Is nice to play football.
      explanation: English structures with the dummy subjects it and there are difficult. The equivalent M/I structure uses the word ada (got/has/is).
      etiology: interlingual
      flagged: false
      tier: 3
    - pattern: Omission of dummy 'it' in impersonal expressions
      example: Is nice to play football.
      frequency: 3
      persistence: 3
      communicative_impact: 2
      example_l1: Enak bermain sepak bola
      example_gloss: Nice play football
      example_wrong: Is nice to play football.
      example_correct: It is nice to play football.
      explanation: M/I can express impersonal statements without a dummy subject. English requires 'it' or 'there'.
      etiology: interlingual
      flagged: false
      tier: 3
    examples:
    - wrong: A few people in the room.
      correct: There are a few people in the room.
    - wrong: Was an examination last week.
      correct: There was an examination last week.
    - wrong: Is nice to play football.
      correct: It is nice to play football.
    why_it_happens: English structures with the dummy subjects it and there are difficult. The equivalent M/I structure uses the word ada (got/has/is). Errors such as these are common. (Swan & Smith, p. 285)
    teacher_tips:
      how_to_explain: 'Teach: English needs a subject. Use ''there is/are'' for existence. Use ''it is'' for impersonal expressions. M/I uses ada; English needs the dummy subject.'
      where_to_start: Start with there is/are. Then teach impersonal it. Then existential vs locative distinctions.
      sequencing: 'Week 1: There is/are. Week 2: Impersonal it. Week 3: It vs there. Week 4: Review.'
      exercises:
      - name: existential_there Audit
        type: error-correction
        description: 20 sentences with existential there errors from M/I learner writing.
        duration: 15
      - name: existential_there Translation
        type: translation
        description: Translate 15 sentences focusing on existential there.
        duration: 15
      - name: existential_there Practice
        type: fill-in-the-blank
        description: 20 sentences practicing existential there.
        duration: 10
      - name: existential_there Error Hunt
        type: error-correction
        description: A paragraph with 10 existential there errors.
        duration: 15
    sources:
    - Swan & Smith Learner English (Indonesian)
    - Parrott Grammar for English Language Teachers
    source_count: 2
    source_type: textbook
    frequency: 4
    persistence: 4
    communicative_impact: 3
    flagged: false
    tier: 1
    source_rank: 1
    citations:
    - claim: English structures with dummy subjects it and there are difficult; M/I uses ada
      source: Swan & Smith (2001) Learner English, 2nd ed., Cambridge University Press
      location: Malay/Indonesian speakers, p. 285
      tier: 1
    - claim: Errors include *A few people in the room, *Is nice to play football, *Was an examination last week
      source: Swan & Smith (2001) Learner English, 2nd ed., Cambridge University Press
      location: Malay/Indonesian speakers, p. 285
      tier: 1
  reflexive_pronouns:
    interference_patterns:
    - pattern: Reflexive pronoun form errors
      example: Ali hurt heself.
      frequency: 3
      persistence: 3
      communicative_impact: 2
      example_l1: Ali melukai dirinya
      example_gloss: Ali hurt self-his
      example_wrong: Ali hurt heself. / They cook for their selves. / We must listen to ourself.
      explanation: The literal translation of the reflexive self is diri in M/I. Learners find the English reflexive pronoun system complicated.
      etiology: interlingual
      flagged: false
      tier: 3
    - pattern: Intensive pronoun errors
      example: Me, myself do all the work.
      frequency: 3
      persistence: 3
      communicative_impact: 2
      example_l1: Saya, diri saya melakukan semua pekerjaan
      example_gloss: I, self-my do all work
      example_wrong: Me, myself do all the work.
      example_correct: I myself do all the work.
      explanation: M/I uses diri in ways that do not map directly to English reflexive/intensive pronouns.
      etiology: interlingual
      flagged: false
      tier: 3
    examples:
    - wrong: Ali hurt heself.
      correct: Ali hurt himself.
    - wrong: They cook for their selves.
      correct: They cook for themselves.
    - wrong: We must listen to ourself.
      correct: We must listen to ourselves.
    - wrong: Me, myself do all the work.
      correct: I myself do all the work.
    why_it_happens: The literal translation of the reflexive self is diri in M/I. Learners find the English reflexive pronoun system complicated. (Swan & Smith, p. 284)
    teacher_tips:
      how_to_explain: 'Teach: myself, yourself, himself, herself, itself, ourselves, yourselves, themselves. Used when subject and object are the same.'
      where_to_start: Start with the forms. Then teach reflexive vs intensive use.
      sequencing: 'Week 1: Reflexive pronoun forms. Week 2: Reflexive vs intensive use. Week 3: Common verbs with reflexives. Week 4: Review.'
      exercises:
      - name: reflexive_pronouns Audit
        type: error-correction
        description: 20 sentences with reflexive pronoun errors from M/I learner writing.
        duration: 15
      - name: reflexive_pronouns Translation
        type: translation
        description: Translate 15 sentences focusing on reflexive pronouns.
        duration: 15
      - name: reflexive_pronouns Practice
        type: fill-in-the-blank
        description: 20 sentences practicing reflexive pronouns.
        duration: 10
      - name: reflexive_pronouns Error Hunt
        type: error-correction
        description: A paragraph with 10 reflexive pronoun errors.
        duration: 15
    sources:
    - Swan & Smith Learner English (Indonesian)
    - Parrott Grammar for English Language Teachers
