    persistence: 3
    communicative_impact: 2
    flagged: false
    tier: 1
    source_rank: 1
    citations:
    - claim: The reflexive self is diri in M/I; learners find English system complicated
      source: Swan & Smith (2001) Learner English, 2nd ed., Cambridge University Press
      location: Malay/Indonesian speakers, p. 284
      tier: 1
    - claim: Errors include *Ali hurt heself, *They cook for their selves, *Me, myself do all the work
      source: Swan & Smith (2001) Learner English, 2nd ed., Cambridge University Press
      location: Malay/Indonesian speakers, p. 284
      tier: 1
  conditionals:
    interference_patterns:
    - pattern: Conditional structure transfer from M/I
      example: If I would have money, I would travel.
      frequency: 3
      persistence: 3
      communicative_impact: 3
      example_l1: Kalau saya punya uang, saya akan jalan-jalan
      example_gloss: If I have money, I will travel
      example_wrong: If I would have money, I would travel.
      example_correct: If I had money, I would travel.
      explanation: M/I conditional structure differs from English. M/I uses 'kalau' with present or akan forms; English requires specific tense patterns in if-clauses.
      etiology: interlingual
      flagged: false
      tier: 3
    examples:
    - wrong: If I would have money, I would travel.
      correct: If I had money, I would travel.
    why_it_happens: M/I conditional structure differs from English. M/I uses kalau + present/akan for both real and unreal conditions. (Swan & Smith, p. 279)
    teacher_tips:
      how_to_explain: 'Teach: NO WOULD IN IF-CLAUSE. Use ''were'' for unreal situations (If I were...). Zero, 1st, 2nd, 3rd conditional patterns.'
      where_to_start: Start with zero and 1st conditional (real). Then 2nd conditional (unreal present). Then 3rd (unreal past).
      sequencing: 'Week 1: Zero and 1st conditional. Week 2: 2nd conditional. Week 3: 3rd conditional. Week 4: Mixed.'
      exercises:
      - name: conditionals Audit
        type: error-correction
        description: 20 sentences with conditional errors from M/I learner writing.
        duration: 15
      - name: conditionals Translation
        type: translation
        description: Translate 15 sentences focusing on conditionals.
        duration: 15
      - name: conditionals Practice
        type: fill-in-the-blank
        description: 20 sentences practicing conditionals.
        duration: 10
      - name: conditionals Error Hunt
        type: error-correction
        description: A paragraph with 10 conditional errors.
        duration: 15
    sources:
    - Swan & Smith Learner English (Indonesian)
    - Parrott Grammar for English Language Teachers
    source_count: 2
    source_type: textbook
    frequency: 3
    persistence: 3
    communicative_impact: 3
    flagged: false
    tier: 1
    source_rank: 1
    citations:
    - claim: M/I conditional structure differs from English
      source: Swan & Smith (2001) Learner English, 2nd ed., Cambridge University Press
      location: Malay/Indonesian speakers, pp. 279-294
      tier: 1
  false_friends:
    interference_patterns:
    - pattern: Lexical transfer of M/I loanwords with shifted meanings
      example: talipon for telephone, ekonomi for economy
      frequency: 3
      persistence: 3
      communicative_impact: 2
      example_l1: talipon, bankrup, ekonomi, industri, psikologia
      example_gloss: telephone, bankrupt, economy, industry, psychology
      example_wrong: talipon (spelling), ekonomi (spelling), psikologia (spelling)
      explanation: Lexical transfer or borrowing from English to M/I involves loanwords, blends and substitutions. Students may be misled by the M/I spelling of words.
      etiology: interlingual
      flagged: true
      tier: 2
    - pattern: Confusion of English word pairs with single M/I equivalent
      example: live/stay, tell/ask/say
      frequency: 4
      persistence: 4
      communicative_impact: 3
      example_l1: tinggal (live/stay), bilang/minta/kata (tell/ask/say)
      example_gloss: live/stay, tell/ask/say
      example_wrong: I've stayed in my village since I was born. / They tell me where is the canteen. / She sends her children to school every day.
      example_correct: I've lived in my village since I was born. / They ask me where the canteen is. / She takes her children to school every day.
      explanation: M/I learners have considerable problems distinguishing pairs of English words that have a single M/I equivalent (e.g., tinggal = live/stay).
      etiology: interlingual
      flagged: true
      tier: 2
    examples:
    - wrong: I've stayed in my village since I was born.
      correct: I've lived in my village since I was born.
    - wrong: They tell me where is the canteen.
      correct: They ask me where the canteen is.
    - wrong: She sends her children to school every day.
      correct: She takes her children to school every day.
    - wrong: Borrow me two dollars.
      correct: Lend me two dollars.
    - wrong: I found everywhere for the cat.
      correct: I looked everywhere for the cat.
    - wrong: I listened a sound in the dark.
      correct: I heard a sound in the dark.
    why_it_happens: M/I learners have considerable problems distinguishing pairs of English words that have a single M/I equivalent. Lexical transfer from English to M/I involves loanwords with different spellings. (Swan & Smith, pp. 290-291)
    teacher_tips:
      how_to_explain: 'Teach: Many English words map to one M/I word. Learn the differences: live vs stay, tell vs ask vs say, lend vs borrow, look vs find, hear vs listen.'
      where_to_start: Start with the most common confusions (live/stay, lend/borrow, look/find). Then teach loanword spelling differences.
      sequencing: 'Week 1: live/stay, tell/ask/say. Week 2: lend/borrow, look/find. Week 3: hear/listen, take/send. Week 4: Loanword spelling.'
      exercises:
      - name: false_friends Audit
        type: error-correction
        description: 20 sentences with false friends errors from M/I learner writing.
        duration: 15
      - name: false_friends Translation
        type: translation
        description: Translate 15 sentences focusing on false friends.
        duration: 15
      - name: false_friends Practice
        type: fill-in-the-blank
        description: 20 sentences practicing false friends.
        duration: 10
      - name: false_friends Error Hunt
        type: error-correction
        description: A paragraph with 10 false friends errors.
        duration: 15
    sources:
    - Swan & Smith Learner English (Indonesian)
    - Parrott Grammar for English Language Teachers
    source_count: 2
