      tier: 1
  vocabulary_style:
    interference_patterns:
    - pattern: Reduced vocabulary range; avoidance of idiomatic expressions
      example: very very proud (for putting on airs)
      frequency: 3
      persistence: 3
      communicative_impact: 2
      example_l1: sangat sangat bangga
      example_gloss: very very proud
      example_wrong: very very proud / to pass very well / very good food / after I finish doing the course
      explanation: Even advanced M/I learners may use a relatively reduced range of vocabulary, avoiding idiomatic expressions in favour of more basic forms.
      etiology: interlingual
      flagged: false
      tier: 2
    - pattern: Tautology for emphasis (M/I discourse feature)
      example: You just merely cycle along.
      frequency: 3
      persistence: 3
      communicative_impact: 2
      example_l1: Kamu cukup saja bersepeda
      example_gloss: You just enough cycle
      example_wrong: You just merely cycle along. / the short cut way home
      explanation: Tautology is commonly used to show emphasis in M/I, and this may be reflected in students' use of English.
      etiology: interlingual
      flagged: false
      tier: 2
    - pattern: Long-windedness (circumlocution)
      example: my mother stays at home only and doesn't go to work
      frequency: 3
      persistence: 3
      communicative_impact: 1
      example_l1: ibu saya di rumah saja dan tidak bekerja
      example_gloss: my mother at home only and not work
      example_wrong: my mother stays at home only and doesn't go to work. / I will further my studies to specialise.
      explanation: M/I tends toward circumlocution. English prefers concise expressions (housewife, specialise).
      etiology: interlingual
      flagged: false
      tier: 2
    examples:
    - wrong: very very proud
      correct: putting on airs
    - wrong: to pass very well
      correct: to pass with flying colours
    - wrong: very good food
      correct: a delicious meal
    - wrong: after I finish doing the course
      correct: after I graduate
    - wrong: You just merely cycle along.
      correct: You simply cycle along.
    - wrong: the short cut way home
      correct: the shortcut home
    - wrong: my mother stays at home only and doesn't go to work.
      correct: my mother is a housewife.
    why_it_happens: Even advanced M/I learners may use a relatively reduced range of vocabulary. Tautology is commonly used to show emphasis in M/I. This contributes to a tendency to long-windedness. (Swan & Smith, pp. 290-291)
    teacher_tips:
      how_to_explain: 'Teach: English prefers concise, idiomatic expressions. Avoid tautology (just merely = redundant). Learn common idioms and collocations.'
      where_to_start: Start with common tautology errors. Then teach idiomatic alternatives. Then vocabulary expansion.
      sequencing: 'Week 1: Tautology errors. Week 2: Idiomatic expressions. Week 3: Concise writing. Week 4: Review.'
      exercises:
      - name: vocabulary_style Audit
        type: error-correction
        description: 20 sentences with vocabulary/style errors from M/I learner writing.
        duration: 15
      - name: vocabulary_style Translation
        type: translation
        description: Translate 15 sentences focusing on vocabulary/style.
        duration: 15
      - name: vocabulary_style Practice
        type: fill-in-the-blank
        description: 20 sentences practicing vocabulary/style.
        duration: 10
      - name: vocabulary_style Error Hunt
        type: error-correction
        description: A paragraph with 10 vocabulary/style errors.
        duration: 15
    sources:
    - Swan & Smith Learner English (Indonesian)
    - Parrott Grammar for English Language Teachers
